The platypus is an egg-laying mammal native to Australia and Tasmania. It has a venomous spur (in males), webbed feet, and is an adept swimmer. It belongs to the rare order Monotremata, shared only with the echidna. Its eggs are leathery and incubated in burrows. The platypus is still relatively mysterious, with much about its behavior and population dynamics yet to be understood.

