er the king and seize the throne. His actions lead to a spiral of violence, paranoia, and ultimately his downfall."}, {"role": "user", "content": "The quotes from his plays are quite famous too right?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes, many quotes from Shakespeare\u2019s plays are famous and often quoted in literature, media, and everyday language. For example:\n\n- *Macbeth*: \u201cTomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow...\u201d  \n- *Romeo and Juliet*: \u201cWhat\u2019s in a name? That which we call a rose by any other name would smell as sweet.\u201d  \n- *Hamlet*: \u201cTo be, or not to be, that is the question.\u201d  \n- *A Midsummer Night\u2019s Dream*: \u201cThe course of true love never did run smooth.\u201d  \n\nThese lines have become cultural touchstones, often used to express deep emotions or philosophical ideas. Let me know if you'd like to explore specific quotes or their meanings!"}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is meant by 'to be or not to b, that is the question'?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The line \u201cTo be, or not to be, that is the question\u201d is from Shakespeare\u2019s *Hamlet*. It reflects the protagonist\u2019s deep internal struggle with life, death, and the uncertainty of what lies beyond. Hamlet is contemplating whether it is nobler to endure life\u2019s suffering or to end it through death. The quote captures existential doubt and the weight of making a life-altering decision. It\u2019s often interpreted as a universal expression of human anguish and philosophical inquiry."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What was the story of Hamlet"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "*Hamlet* is one of Shakespeare\u2019s most famous tragedies. It follows Prince Hamlet of Denmark, who is devastated by his father\u2019s sudden death and his mother\u2019s quick remarriage to his uncle, Claudius. The ghost of Hamlet\u2019s father appears, revealing that Claudius murdered him. Hamlet feigns madness to investigate and seek revenge, but his indecision and moral conflict lead to tragic consequences, including the deaths of several characters, including himself. The play explores themes of betrayal, revenge, madness, and existential doubt."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is indecision?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Indecision is the state of being unable to make a choice or come to a decision. It often involves hesitation, doubt, or overthinking about the possible outcomes of different options. In *Hamlet*, the title character is famously indecisive, which drives much of the play\u2019s tension and tragedy. His inability to act on his desire for revenge leads to delays, misunderstandings, and ultimately, the downfall of many characters. Indecision can stem from fear, uncertainty, or a lack of confidence in one\u2019s choices."}, {"role": "user", "content": "User question:\nWillim was also known for his sonnets i guess?\n\nRelevant context:\nUser preferences:\n- The user prefers concise answers.\n- The user is open to exploring their emotions and seeking help.\n- The user likes long, detailed answers.\n- The user prefers teeny tiny answers.\n- The user prefers that each platypus fact be accompanied by an explanation.\n\nKnown user facts:\n- The user feels unheard and frustrated when they have to fight for their needs to be met.\n- The user feels like they deserve care, space, and people who show up for them without them having to ask.\n- The user feels like they deserve to be heard, supported, and included without having to fight for it.\n- The user feels like they have to question whether their family truly has their back.\n- The user feels like they have to question whether anyone truly understands them.\n\nActive user goals:\n- The user is building a chatbot with pgvector.\n- The user wants to explore shadow work further.\n- The user wants the assistant to provide a platypus fact with an explanation each time they say \"next\".\n- The user has requested multiple new platypus facts, i