sophical inquiry.\nuser: What was the story of Hamlet\nassistant: *Hamlet* is one of Shakespeare\u2019s most famous tragedies. It follows Prince Hamlet of Denmark, who is devastated by his father\u2019s sudden death and his mother\u2019s quick remarriage to his uncle, Claudius. The ghost of Hamlet\u2019s father appears, revealing that Claudius murdered him. Hamlet feigns madness to investigate and seek revenge, but his indecision and moral conflict lead to tragic consequences, including the deaths of several characters, including himself. The play explores themes of betrayal, revenge, madness, and existential doubt.\nuser: What is indecision?\nassistant: Indecision is the state of being unable to make a choice or come to a decision. It often involves hesitation, doubt, or overthinking about the possible outcomes of different options. In *Hamlet*, the title character is famously indecisive, which drives much of the play\u2019s tension and tragedy. His inability to act on his desire for revenge leads to delays, misunderstandings, and ultimately, the downfall of many characters. Indecision can stem from fear, uncertainty, or a lack of confidence in one\u2019s choices.\nuser: Willim was also known for his sonnets i guess?\nassistant: Yes, William Shakespeare is also well known for his sonnets. He wrote 154 sonnets, which are 14-line poems typically written in iambic pentameter. They explore themes like love, beauty, time, and mortality. The sonnets are divided into two main groups: the first 126 are addressed to a young man, and the later ones to a mysterious \"Dark Lady.\" They are celebrated for their emotional depth and poetic craftsmanship.\nuser: Can you give sonet 29?\nassistant: Sure! Here is Sonnet 29 by William Shakespeare:\n\n**Sonnet 29**  \nWhen, in disgrace with fortune and men\u2019s eyes,  \nI all alone do grieve my thoughts, and weep,  \nTo think I may not always pleas\u2019d in thought,  \nNor ever after love the sweet use of use  \nSo long as I can see