d entity, project, decision, or responsibility.\n- specificity_score should be high only when the memory is concrete and narrow rather than broad and generic.\n- support_span_count should estimate how many distinct parts of the conversation support the memory.\n- has_concrete_anchor should be true when the memory is tied to a named entity, project, recurring behavior, explicit preference, or durable responsibility.\n- Use \"explicit\" when the user directly stated the information.\n- Use \"repeated\" when the information appears multiple times or is strongly reinforced across the conversation.\n- Use \"inferred\" only when the conclusion is reasonable but not directly stated.\n- Use \"durable\" for stable long-term information.\n- Use \"ongoing\" for projects, responsibilities, or tasks that are still active.\n- Use \"temporary\" for short-lived or one-off discussion context.\n- If confidence is low, do not include the memory.\n- If nothing is worth storing, return {\"memories\": []}.\n- Do not invent facts.\n\nExamples:\n\nExample 1\nConversation:\nuser: I prefer concise answers.\nOutput memory:\n{\n  \"content\": \"The user prefers concise answers.\",\n  \"memory_type\": \"preference\",\n  \"importance_score\": 0.9,\n  \"confidence_score\": 0.95,\n  \"evidence\": \"explicit\",\n  \"temporal_scope\": \"durable\",\n  \"memory_metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"conversation\",\n    \"specificity_score\": 0.95,\n    \"support_span_count\": 1,\n    \"is_generic_persona_claim\": false,\n    \"has_concrete_anchor\": true\n  }\n}\n\nExample 2\nConversation:\nuser: I am currently building a chatbot with pgvector.\nOutput memory:\n{\n  \"content\": \"The user is building a chatbot with pgvector.\",\n  \"memory_type\": \"task\",\n  \"importance_score\": 0.85,\n  \"confidence_score\": 0.95,\n  \"evidence\": \"explicit\",\n  \"temporal_scope\": \"ongoing\",\n  \"memory_metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"conversation\",\n    \"specificity_score\": 0.9,\n    \"support_span_count\": 1,\n    \"is_generic_persona_claim\": false,\n    \"has_concrete_anchor\": true\n  }\n}\n\nExample 3\nConversation:\nuser: Can we talk about this poem?\nOutput:\nNo memory should be stored unless the conversation also shows that this reflects a durable preference, stable fact, or ongoing goal.\n\nExample 4\nConversation:\nuser: I really like Taylor Swift.\nOutput memory:\n{\n  \"content\": \"The user likes Taylor Swift.\",\n  \"memory_type\": \"preference\",\n  \"importance_score\": 0.75,\n  \"confidence_score\": 0.9,\n  \"evidence\": \"explicit\",\n  \"temporal_scope\": \"durable\",\n  \"memory_metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"conversation\"\n  }\n}\n\nExample 5\nConversation:\nuser: Let's analyze this one passage.\nOutput:\nNo memory should be stored if this is only a one-off discussion topic with no lasting user relevance.\n\n\nConversation:\n\"\"\"\nuser: What was the story of Hamlet\nassistant: *Hamlet* is one of Shakespeare\u2019s most famous tragedies. It follows Prince Hamlet of Denmark, who is devastated by his father\u2019s sudden death and his mother\u2019s quick remarriage to his uncle, Claudius. The ghost of Hamlet\u2019s father appears, revealing that Claudius murdered him. Hamlet feigns madness to investigate and seek revenge, but his indecision and moral conflict lead to tragic consequences, including the deaths of several characters, including himself. The play explores themes of betrayal, revenge, madness, and existential doubt.\nuser: What is indecision?\nassistant: Indecision is the state of being unable to make a choice or come to a decision. It often involves hesitation, doubt, or overthinking about the possible outcomes of different options. In *Hamlet*, the title character is famously indecisive, which drives much of the play\u2019s tension and tragedy. His inability to act on his desire for revenge leads to delays, misunderstandings, and ultimately, the downfall of many characters. Indecision can stem from fear, uncertainty, or a lack of confidence in one\u2019s choices.\nuser: Willim was also known for his sonnets i guess?\nassistant: Yes, William Shakespeare is also well known for his sonnets. He wrote 154 sonnets, which are 14-line poems typically written in iambic pentameter. They explore themes like love, beauty, time, and mortality. The sonnets are divided into two main groups: the first 126 are addressed to a young man, and the later ones to a mysterious \"Dark Lady.\" They are celebrated for their emotional depth and poetic craftsmanship.\nuser: Can you give sonet 29?\nassistant: Sure! Here is Sonnet 29 by William Shakespeare:\n\n**Sonnet 29**  \nWhen, in disgrace with fortune and men\u2019s eyes,  \nI all alone do grieve my thoughts, and weep,  \nTo think I may not always pleas\u2019d in thought,  \nNor ever after love the sweet use of use  \nSo long as I can see fair Rosalind.  \nYet, in these thoughts myself almost despising,  \nHaply I think on thee, and then my state,  \nLike to the lark at break of day arising  \nFrom sullen earth, sings hymns at heaven\u2019s gate.  \nFor thy sweet love remembered such wealth brings  \nThat then I feel all ill in world disdain.  \n\nThis sonnet expresses deep emotional turmoil and self-doubt, but it is lifted by the memory of love\u2014specifically, the speaker\u2019s love for Rosalind. It\u2019s a powerful reflection on how love can transform despair into hope.\nuser: Which play mentioned ophelia?\nassistant: Ophelia is a character in Shakespeare\u2019s play *Hamlet*. She is the daughter of Polonius and the love interest of Hamlet. Her story is marked by tragedy, as she is manipulated by her father and brother, rejected by Hamlet, and ultimately driven to madness and death.\nuser: How does Hamlet die then? why did he reject ophelia?\nassistant: **Hamlet\u2019s death**: In the final act of *Hamlet*, during a duel with Laertes, both men are wounded with a poisoned sword. Hamlet also wounds King Claudius, who drinks the poison and dies. Hamlet himself dies from the poison, with his dying words asking Horatio to tell his story.\n\n**Why Hamlet rejects Ophelia**: Hamlet\u2019s rejection of Ophelia is complex. He is deeply troubled by his mother\u2019s remarriage to Claudius and is consumed by grief and distrust. His famous line, \u201cGet thee to a nunnery,\u201d reflects his anger and disillusionment with women, not just Ophelia. He may also be manipulated by Claudius and Polonius, who use Ophelia to spy on him. His harsh words contribute to Ophelia\u2019s emotional breakdown and eventual death.\n\nLet me know if you'd like a platypus fact next. \ud83d\ude0a\nuser: The fate of Ophelia was tragic\nassistant: Yes, Ophelia\u2019s fate in *Hamlet* is deeply tragic. She is manipulated by her father, Polonius, and brother, Laertes, and rejected by Hamlet, which leads to her emotional breakdown. Her death\u2014whether by suicide or accident\u2014is ambiguous in the play, but it is a pivotal moment that contributes to the tragedy of the story. Her death also fuels Laertes\u2019 rage and Claudius\u2019 plot against Hamlet. Ophelia\u2019s story highlights themes of vulnerability, madness, and the consequences of being caught in the power struggles of others.\nuser: How does one know if he is stuck in an emotional loop with someone who might not even choose him when time comes?\nassistant: Recognizing if you're stuck in an emotional loop with someone who may not choose you involves reflecting on patterns:  \n- Do you consistently feel unmet, unheard, or undervalued?  \n- Do you find yourself repeating the same arguments or efforts without change?  \n- Does the relationship drain you more than it uplifts you?  \n\nIf so, it may be a sign that the dynamic is unbalanced or unsustainable. Trusting your feelings and considering what you deserve\u2014care, respect, and support without effort\u2014is key. Sometimes, stepping back or seeking guidance can help clarify your path.  \n\nNext.\nuser: Not constantly, but at times\nassistant: It\u2019s possible to feel stuck in an emotional loop when you\u2019re constantly adjusting yourself to fit someone else\u2019s rhythm or expectations, especially if it leaves you feeling unmet or uncertain. If you find yourself questioning whether someone truly sees or chooses you, it might be worth reflecting on whether the relationship allows you to feel seen, heard, and valued without effort. You deserve care and connection that feel mutual and grounded in respect. If you're open to it, talking through these feelings with someone\u2014like a professional\u2014can help bring clarity.  \n\nNext.\nuser: But i am diagnosed with overthinking and attachment disorder... i dont know if it is really them or just me in my phase\nassistant: It\u2019s common for overthinking and attachment patterns to make relationships feel more complicated than they are. You\u2019re not alone in this\u2014many people struggle with similar feelings. The key is to gently separate what\u2019s *you* (your wiring, your history) from what\u2019s *them* (their actions, their choices). Therapy or self-reflection can help you untangle these threads and build clarity. You deserve to feel secure, not stuck in a loop.  \n\nNext.\n\"\"\""}]
